How to Enforce It: When evaluating branches in your Tree of Thought, inject a mandate: “Select the second-best branch that we ultimately discarded. Simulate a scenario where our primary choice fails immediately due to an environmental shift. Map out a 'hot-swappable' bridge from our current active path back into that discarded alternative path.”
How to Enforce It: In your evaluation loop, require a "Black Swan" check: “Identify the consensus approach that all your active branches are currently leaning toward. Now, write a rule that explicitly bans the core mechanism of that consensus. Force yourself to generate one high-viability solution under this restriction.”
How to Enforce It: Before a branch is allowed to commit an action, force it through a bottleneck tool or prompt: “Compress the problem state and your proposed 500-word solution into exactly three 5-word rules. No more, no less. If your rules cannot fully represent the execution logic, your solution is too noisy. Refactor it.”
How to Enforce It: “Do not evaluate if your code or answer is correct. Evaluate the cognitive friction it introduces to the human interacting with it. Rank your branches based on how much mental energy the user must expend to understand, maintain, or verify your output. Prune the highest-friction branches.”

